      {
        "text": "Give each tenant an isolated runtime with its own workspace, memory, configuration, and outbound credential inputs.",
        "explanation": "Authentication and transcript labels do not isolate the subprocess's other state sources. Tenant separation must cover the filesystem, configuration, and access paths the agent can actually use."
      },
      {
        "text": "Add the authenticated tenant name to every system prompt while retaining the shared runtime environment.",
        "explanation": "A prompt label guides behavior but does not prevent the runtime from exposing another tenant's files, memory, settings, or credentials."
      },
      {
        "text": "Store transcripts in separate database tables while preserving the shared workspace to simplify artifact retrieval.",
        "explanation": "Separating transcript storage addresses only one state store. The shared filesystem and configuration still permit cross-tenant context or capability leakage."
      },
      {
        "text": "Filter tenant names from final answers and use response monitoring to detect any residual cross-client references.",
        "explanation": "Output filtering cannot reliably undo unauthorized reads or tool actions. The isolation requirement must be enforced before shared state reaches the agent."
      }
