    "prompt": "A framework-based prototype performs a fixed three-call workflow correctly, but its abstractions hide rendered prompts, retries, and intermediate responses. The production requirement includes reconstructing failed executions. No framework-only capability is needed. Which actions are justified when selecting the production architecture? Select 2 answers.",
    "answers": [
      {
        "text": "Require access to the underlying prompts, responses, and execution transitions before accepting the framework.",
        "explanation": "Abstractions that obscure execution can defeat the stated reconstruction requirement. Understanding and exposing the underlying behavior is necessary even when final outputs look correct."
      },
      {
        "text": "Treat the framework's final-answer log as sufficient evidence because the acceptance sample produced correct outputs.",
        "explanation": "A final answer does not reveal retries, intermediate failures, or the actual prompts. Passing a sample does not satisfy the separate execution-reconstruction requirement."
      },
      {
        "text": "Add a supervisor agent that narrates what the hidden workflow probably did whenever an execution fails.",
        "explanation": "A generated account is not a trace of the actual hidden execution. Another agent cannot recover evidence that the runtime never exposed."
      },
      {
        "text": "Compare a direct-API implementation of the fixed workflow against the same acceptance and operational requirements.",
        "explanation": "Direct calls may meet the task with fewer obscuring layers. Comparing equivalent requirements tests that alternative without assuming that all frameworks are unsuitable."
      }
    ],
