    "domain": 1,
    "objectiveId": "1.4",
    "difficulty": "advanced",
    "type": "multiple",
    "prompt": "A daily briefing has a fixed release deadline. Its mandatory core evidence is already verified; regional worker reports are optional supplements. Some regional searches stall. The business accepts missing supplements if omissions are disclosed, but it does not accept treating missing evidence as a negative finding. Which orchestration choices satisfy this contract? Select 3 answers.",
    "answers": [
      {
        "text": "Release the verified core by the deadline and disclose which optional supplements were unavailable.",
        "explanation": "The contract permits an explicitly incomplete supplement set. Publishing the valid core with disclosed omissions preserves both the deadline and the evidence boundary."
      },
      {
        "text": "Collect optional worker results without requiring an all-worker completion barrier before publication.",
        "explanation": "An unconditional synchronous barrier makes release depend on the slowest optional worker. Removing that dependency allows useful parallel work without violating the core release requirement."
      },
      {
        "text": "Wait for every regional search and retry stalled workers until each produces a complete report.",
        "explanation": "Unbounded waiting gives optional work control over the release deadline. The business explicitly permits omissions instead of requiring full regional completion."
      },
      {
        "text": "Represent pending and failed supplements explicitly so synthesis cannot interpret silence as evidence of absence.",
        "explanation": "Typed task outcomes distinguish a failed search from a completed search with no finding. This prevents orchestration failures from becoming unsupported business conclusions."
      }
    ],
