    "prompt": "During initial authorization with no previously granted scopes, an HTTP MCP 2026-07-28 server challenges for a tenant-specific scope not listed in its scopes_supported metadata. The client is using this version's protected-resource OAuth flow. Which implementation decisions conform to its scope and token-binding requirements? Select 3 answers.",
    "answers": [
      {
        "text": "Treat the challenge's scope set as authoritative for the current operation rather than assuming it is a subset of scopes_supported.",
        "explanation": "The specification permits challenged scopes to have no fixed subset relationship with scopes_supported. An unlisted tenant-specific scope is not automatically invalid for the operation."
      },
      {
        "text": "Intersect the challenged scopes with scopes_supported and authorize only the intersection.",
        "explanation": "Intersection can remove a scope the server requires for this operation. The client must not assume that scopes_supported exhaustively lists every dynamically challenged scope."
      },
      {
        "text": "Include the canonical MCP resource identifier in both the authorization request and token request.",
        "explanation": "The resource parameter is mandatory at both stages in this version's flow. It identifies the resource for which the client is requesting the access token."
      },
      {
        "text": "Send the resulting bearer token in Authorization on each HTTP request, not in a URL query parameter.",
        "explanation": "Authorization accompanies every client-to-server HTTP request under this flow. The specification prohibits access tokens in URI query strings, where they can leak through URL handling."
      }
    ],
