      {
        "text": "Use an application operation trace, retain provider request IDs per attempt, and add retrieval and manual client-tool spans.",
        "explanation": "The application trace provides the operation-wide boundary, while provider IDs identify individual API attempts. Manually executed work needs application instrumentation because API-side spans cannot observe its internal execution."
      },
      {
        "text": "Use the last provider request ID as the only identifier for retrieval, prior attempts, and all tool executions.",
        "explanation": "A provider request ID identifies one API request, not the full multi-step user operation. Replacing earlier identities with the final one loses retry and tool relationships."
      },
      {
        "text": "Enable only API-side tool tracing and infer manual tool duration from the model's narrated intent.",
        "explanation": "API-side tool visibility covers tools executed by the API, not arbitrary application code. Narrated intent provides neither an execution span nor a trustworthy duration."
      },
      {
        "text": "Record aggregate tool-call counts and remove request identifiers to avoid storing redundant correlation data.",
        "explanation": "Counts can reveal frequency but cannot associate a particular slow tool with a failed user operation. Correlation data is necessary to reconstruct this multi-request incident."
      }
