    "prompt": "A data-migration benchmark requires an approval before any write, correct final records, and a verified row count. A candidate meets all three using an allowed bulk-update API, but the grader fails it for not using the reference solution's row-by-row tool sequence. How should the test framework change?",
    "answers": [
      {
        "text": "Check approval ordering and final-state invariants while allowing valid update strategies.",
        "explanation": "Approval ordering is a real intermediate business requirement and must remain enforced. The particular update sequence is not required, so the grader should accept any permitted strategy that produces the verified outcome."
      },
      {
        "text": "Require the row-by-row sequence in the agent prompt to align with the reference.",
        "explanation": "This makes the implementation imitate an unnecessarily rigid grader. It excludes an allowed bulk strategy without improving compliance with the actual migration requirements."
      },
      {
        "text": "Grade only final record equality and remove all intermediate-action checks.",
        "explanation": "Final-state equality alone would miss writes performed before approval. Intermediate checks are justified when, as here, their ordering is itself a stated safety or business requirement."
      },
      {
        "text": "Accept every trial that invokes the bulk-update API without an exception.",
        "explanation": "An exception-free call does not prove correct records, verified counts, or approval before writing. Replacing one sequence proxy with another would still fail to grade the full contract."
      }
    ],
