    "id": "ccarp-4.3-stateful-workspace-assignment",
    "domain": 4,
    "objectiveId": "4.3",
    "difficulty": "intermediate",
    "type": "single",
    "prompt": "Two assistant variants update a shared workspace memory used by all members of that workspace in later sessions. Workspaces do not share memory. An A/B test should prevent one variant's memory updates from affecting the other's outcomes. What is the appropriate assignment unit?",
    "answers": [
      {
        "text": "Randomize each model request independently within a workspace.",
        "explanation": "Request-level assignment lets both variants read memory changed by the other. Their outcomes would no longer reflect separate treatments."
      },
      {
        "text": "Randomize each user and keep that user's assignment across sessions.",
        "explanation": "User-level persistence does not isolate memory shared with other workspace members. Users in different arms could still change one another's treatment environment."
      },
      {
        "text": "Alternate variants by day while keeping the same workspace memory.",
        "explanation": "Daily switching retains memory effects from earlier days and introduces time-related differences. It does not separate the stateful treatments."
      },
      {
        "text": "Randomize workspaces and keep each workspace on one variant.",
        "explanation": "The workspace is the stated shared-state boundary, so persistent cluster assignment prevents cross-arm memory updates within it. This is an application of cluster randomization, not a complete statistical design; analysis and power must still respect the assignment unit."
      }
    ],
