    "type": "single",
    "prompt": "An assistant repeatedly sends the same long policy and unchanged tools within a supported cache lifetime. The policy meets the model's cache-length requirement, but every request prepends a unique correlation ID to the system text and cache reads remain zero. What should be changed first?",
    "answers": [
      {
        "text": "Choose a longer cache lifetime while keeping the unique ID before the policy.",
        "explanation": "A longer lifetime does not make different prefixes identical. The unique early value prevents reuse even though calls already occur within the supported lifetime."
      },
      {
        "text": "Put the cache breakpoint after the entire message, including its unique user question.",
        "explanation": "A breakpoint after varying content does not create a stable reusable prefix. It leaves the correlation ID and changing question inside the material expected to match."
      },
      {
        "text": "Lower the output budget and retain the existing system-text and cache layout.",
        "explanation": "Output generation settings do not repair an input-prefix mismatch. They can reduce generation length but will not turn the reported misses into policy-prefix hits."
      },
      {
        "text": "Move the ID to the varying suffix, mark the stable prefix, and verify cache counters.",
        "explanation": "Caching requires an identical eligible prefix, ordered through tools, system, and messages. Keeping variable data after the stable policy boundary enables reuse, and usage counters verify whether it actually occurs."
      }
    ],
