    "id": "ccarp-5.5-small-subgroup-uncertainty",
    "domain": 5,
    "objectiveId": "5.5",
    "difficulty": "advanced",
    "type": "multiple",
    "prompt": "A community information service proposes adding a minority language. Eight reviewed cases in that language contain no harmful missed escalations, while thousands of majority-language cases meet the release criterion. Policy requires demonstrated escalation performance for each supported language; a human-assisted route may remain available. Which three responses are justified? Select 3 answers.",
    "answers": [
      {
        "text": "Obtain appropriately collected, representative labeled cases with qualified language reviewers, including difficult dialect and mixed-context requests.",
        "explanation": "Representative evidence and qualified labels address the limited coverage of eight cases. Additional majority-language examples would not establish performance for the proposed language."
      },
      {
        "text": "Report language-specific error estimates with their uncertainty and the high-severity cases that remain untested.",
        "explanation": "No observed harm in a small sample does not establish equivalent performance or satisfy every release criterion. Explicit uncertainty prevents absence of observations from becoming a fairness assurance."
      },
      {
        "text": "Pool all languages and apply the precise overall error estimate to the minority language, since no minority-language failure was observed.",
        "explanation": "The pooled estimate is dominated by majority-language data and does not resolve the small subgroup's uncertainty. It can hide precisely the disparity the policy requires the team to assess."
      },
      {
        "text": "Keep the proposed language on the disclosed human-assisted route until its release evidence is sufficient, while preserving feedback and escalation access.",
        "explanation": "A bounded supported route respects the stated per-language evidence requirement without silently granting unvalidated autonomous operation. Users retain a path to service and reporting while evidence is gathered."
      }
    ],
