    "id": "ccarp-5.5-contextual-fairness-criteria",
    "domain": 5,
    "objectiveId": "5.5",
    "difficulty": "advanced",
    "type": "single",
    "prompt": "An AI-assisted moderation service flags posts for human review. Missed abusive posts expose users to harm, while unnecessary flags delay legitimate speech and consume a constrained review queue. User groups have different observed abuse rates and sample sizes. Equal flagging rates still leave different harmful-error rates. Demographic-specific thresholds require separate policy and privacy approval. What should guide fairness acceptance?",
    "answers": [
      {
        "text": "Require identical overall flagging rates and automatically apply group-specific thresholds until that parity measure is achieved.",
        "explanation": "Equal flagging rates do not resolve different rates of missed abuse or unnecessary restrictions. Automatically changing group-specific thresholds also bypasses the stated approval requirement."
      },
      {
        "text": "Approve context-specific trade-offs using subgroup harm and uncertainty evidence, review burdens, and evaluated mitigations.",
        "explanation": "Fairness depends on the decision context, affected people, error costs, and applicable constraints. Documented criteria and authorized trade-offs are more defensible than treating one parity result as universal proof."
      },
      {
        "text": "Choose the configuration with the highest overall classification accuracy and treat its pooled score as the fairness acceptance criterion.",
        "explanation": "Overall accuracy can prefer a configuration that concentrates harmful errors in a smaller group. It does not resolve missed abuse, delayed legitimate speech, or the distribution of review burdens."
      },
      {
        "text": "Equalize false-alarm rates and treat that parity result as sufficient, even if missed abusive posts remain concentrated in one group.",
        "explanation": "False-alarm parity addresses one relevant burden but does not establish fairness across all stated harms. Acceptance must also consider missed abuse, uncertainty, context, and authorized trade-offs."
      }
    ],
