    "id": "ccarp-7.2-fail-closed-hook-contract",
    "domain": 7,
    "objectiveId": "7.2",
    "difficulty": "advanced",
    "type": "single",
    "prompt": "On Linux with Claude Code 2.1.304, a synchronous PreToolUse command hook matches every tool call relevant to a target policy. It emits no stdout and exits 1 for a forbidden target. It also occasionally fails to start or times out. Calls have proceeded after these failures. The requirement is to block both a policy denial and a failed policy evaluator. Which correction fits the documented contract?",
    "answers": [
      {
        "text": "Keep exit 1 for denials and increase the timeout, since every nonzero status is a blocking verdict at PreToolUse.",
        "explanation": "With no decision JSON, nonzero statuses other than 2 normally report non-blocking errors. A longer timeout does not change that exit contract or cover a missing executable."
      },
      {
        "text": "Return exit 2 for denials and leave failure handling unset, since start failures and timeouts automatically inherit the denial status.",
        "explanation": "Exit 2 blocks an explicit PreToolUse denial, but a script that never starts or times out may never return that verdict. Those evaluator failures need the supported fail-closed configuration."
      },
      {
        "text": "Return exit 2 for policy denials, set onFailure to block on the synchronous gate, and test denial, missing-executable, and timeout cases.",
        "explanation": "PreToolUse exit 2 rejects the call, and onFailure: block covers evaluator failures on this supported event. The option requires v2.1.295 or later, so the stated 2.1.304 version qualifies."
      },
      {
        "text": "Set onFailure to block and run the hook asynchronously, preserving the policy decision without delaying tool execution.",
        "explanation": "The documented fail-closed option has no effect on background command hooks. An asynchronous check cannot both let the call proceed immediately and serve as its required blocking evaluator."
      }
    ],
